# Server API — for the VS Code "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client).
# Click "Send Request" top to bottom; named responses feed the variables automatically.
#
# {{server}} comes from the environment picked in VS Code's status bar (local, test —
# defined in .vscode/settings.json, named per ARCHITECTURE.md's environment glossary).
# Outside that workspace setup, uncomment a line instead:
# @server = http://localhost:5080

@email = you@example.com
@password = Sup3rSecret!

### Health check
GET {{server}}/healthz

### 1. Register an account (once per email)
#    Only where registration is open: Development (local) is, standing environments are
#    closed by default and answer 403 — flip Registration__Enabled on the stamp to open one.
POST {{server}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{email}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

### 2. Log in — everything below picks the token up from this response
#    NOTE (local only; test's accounts persist in its managed PostgreSQL):
#    after `docker compose down -v` the accounts are gone — re-send
#    register + login so you're not holding a token for a deleted user.
#    Five wrong passwords lock the account for five minutes (401 either way,
#    with the reason in the ProblemDetails body), and this endpoint answers at
#    most 20 times a minute per caller — send it in a loop and you get 429.
# @name login
POST {{server}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{email}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

###
@token = {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### 3. Pair a machine — paste the code the Daemon shows (local: docker compose logs daemon)
#    (skip if this Daemon is already paired; codes are one-time and expire in 10 min)
@pairingCode = PASTE_CODE_HERE

# @name claim
POST {{server}}/api/enrollment/claim
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "code": "{{pairingCode}}", "displayName": "Compose Daemon" }

### 4. My machines — online status appears ~5s after a claim
# @name machines
GET {{server}}/api/daemons
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

###
@registrationId = {{machines.response.body.$[0].registrationId}}

### 5. What the machine shares (the valid starting points for every path below)
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/roots
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### List a directory through the relay
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/data
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Subfolder
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/data/src
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Outside the shared roots → 400
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6. Download a file through the relay (streamed in chunks, never buffered whole)
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/download?path=/data/README.md
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 7. Upload a file (streams request body -> Server -> Daemon; /uploads is the writable root)
#    Transfers are also bounded per user: a daily byte quota and a bandwidth cap
#    (TransferLimits in appsettings) — past the quota, transfer endpoints answer 429.
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/upload?path=/uploads/hello.txt&overwrite=true
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

Hello from Server.http

### Uploading into a read-only root is refused → 400
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/upload?path=/data/hello.txt
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

nope

### Without overwrite, an existing file is left alone → 400 (send #7 first)
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/upload?path=/uploads/hello.txt
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

should not land

### Confirm the upload landed
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/uploads
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### How much of today's transfer allowance is spent (bytes; limit 0 = none configured)
GET {{server}}/api/transfers/usage
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 8. Copy a file machine-to-machine (source and target may be the same machine)
#    Bytes go source -> Server -> target, never buffered whole, no client round trip.
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/copy-to/{{registrationId}}?sourcePath=/data/README.md&targetPath=/uploads/README-copy.md&overwrite=true
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 8b. Make a directory (writable roots only)
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/mkdir?path=/uploads/new-folder
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Rename = move with the same parent (overwrite=true replaces an existing FILE target)
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/move?sourcePath=/uploads/hello.txt&targetPath=/uploads/renamed.txt
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Delete a file, or a directory with everything in it
DELETE {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/entries?path=/uploads/renamed.txt
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Mutations in a read-only root are refused → 400
POST {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/mkdir?path=/data/nope
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### A shared root itself can never be deleted → 400
DELETE {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/entries?path=/uploads
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Sandbox refusal — outside every allowed root → 400
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/etc
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The Daemon's own state is refused even if a root were to contain it → 400
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/download?path=/state/daemon.key
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Traversal is canonicalized before the root check → 400
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/data/../etc
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### No token → 401
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}/list?path=/data

### Someone else's / unknown machine → 404
GET {{server}}/api/daemons/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/list?path=/data
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 9. Unpair a machine → 204. It vanishes from the list and every relay op answers 404;
#     a connected Daemon discards its registration and prints a fresh pairing code.
DELETE {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Unpairing it again (or any unknown machine) → 404
DELETE {{server}}/api/daemons/{{registrationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 10. Personal access tokens — what MCP clients hold. The value appears ONCE, here.
# @name createPat
POST {{server}}/api/pats
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Server.http experiment" }

###
@pat = {{createPat.response.body.$.token}}

### The tokens, listed (values withheld — only hashes exist anymore)
GET {{server}}/api/pats
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 11. The MCP endpoint speaks JSON-RPC over Streamable HTTP; a PAT is its only key
POST {{server}}/mcp
Authorization: Bearer {{pat}}
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list","params":{}}

### Call a tool — the same relay, sandbox and quotas as every other client
POST {{server}}/mcp
Authorization: Bearer {{pat}}
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"list_machines","arguments":{}}}

### A PAT is not a REST credential (and a user JWT is not an MCP one) → 403
GET {{server}}/api/daemons
Authorization: Bearer {{pat}}

### The scheme is case-insensitive, as RFC 7235 says it is — this is the same call, accepted
POST {{server}}/mcp
Authorization: bearer {{pat}}
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/list","params":{}}

### 12. Revoke the token → 204. Needs a user token: a PAT can never mint or revoke PATs
DELETE {{server}}/api/pats/{{createPat.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Revocation is instant — no expiry to wait out. Send #11 again to watch it die → 401
POST {{server}}/mcp
Authorization: Bearer {{pat}}
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"method":"tools/list","params":{}}

# Not included: /api/enrollment/code, /api/enrollment/status and /api/daemon-auth/* —
# those are called by the Daemon itself (the token one needs an ECDsa signature you
# can't produce in an .http file). Watch them happen in: docker compose logs daemon
